course: dictionary
lesson: 6367
title: Much of a muchness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Muchness is an old, rare word. It means greatness in amount or size. You hardly ever hear it on its own today.
explain: Today you mostly meet it in one phrase: «much of a muchness». It means nearly the same, with little difference. Use the context: if the phrase is about two things, they are probably alike.
explain: In Alice in Wonderland, the Dormouse makes a joke about it. He talks of drawing «mouse-traps, and the moon, and memory, and muchness», and you cannot draw muchness. The joke is about the word, not a picture.
fact: Muchness is a noun, and it is uncountable, so you cannot say one muchness or two muchnesses.
fact: «Muchness» means greatness in amount or size. This meaning is old and rare.
fact: «Much of a muchness» is a UK phrase. It means nearly the same, with little difference.
fact: Phrases can mean something different from their single words. «Much of a muchness» does not mean that there is a lot of something.
fact: In Alice in Wonderland, the Dormouse draws «everything that begins with an M». Muchness is a joke in that story, because you cannot draw it.
exercise: «The two shops are much of a muchness.» What does this mean? | They are very far apart | *They are nearly the same, with little difference | They are both very old | They are both closed on Sundays
exercise: In an old book, a writer talks about the muchness of the sea. What does muchness mean? | Small and tidy | *Greatness in amount or size | A tiny drop | Being a mouse
exercise: In Alice in Wonderland, what is the joke about muchness? | You draw it with a pencil and a ruler | It is the same as the moon | *You cannot draw muchness, because it is a word and not a thing you can draw | It is the name of a mouse
test: «Your two drawings are much of a muchness.» What does the phrase suggest? | *They look nearly the same | They are both finished in a hurry, and one is missing | They are the biggest drawings in the room | They were drawn by a mouse
test: Mia asks, «Is muchness a word I can use all the time?» What is the best answer? | No, it is only a name for a mouse | Yes, you can use it every day to mean a lot | *It is old and rare, but you will meet it in the phrase «much of a muchness» | Yes, it means happy
exam: Which sentence uses the phrase «much of a muchness» correctly? | *The two bikes are much of a muchness, since they are nearly identical | The two bikes are much of a muchness, both red and both the same size | The two bikes are much of a muchness, one is a train | The two bikes are much of a muchness, because it is raining
